give an example of a try catch block in java Here is an example of a try-catch block in Java:

```java
public class TryCatchExample {

	public static void main(String[] args) {
		try {
			// Code that might throw an exception
			int number1 = 10;
			int number2 = 0;
			int result = number1 / number2;
			System.out.println("Result: " + result);
		} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
			// Exception handler for ArithmeticException
			System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero!");
		}
	}
}
```

In this example, the try block contains code that divides `number1` by `number2`. However, dividing by zero will throw an `ArithmeticException`. To handle this potential exception, a catch block is used to catch and handle the exception. If an `ArithmeticException` is thrown, the message "Cannot divide by zero!" will be printed. with a conditional Certainly! Here's an example of a try-catch block with a conditional statement in Java:

```java
public class TryCatchConditionalExample {

	public static void main(String[] args) {
		int[] numbers = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
		int index = 6; // Index that is out of bounds

		try {
			// Code that might throw an exception
			int result = numbers[index];
			System.out.println("Value at index " + index + ": " + result);
		} catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
			// Exception handler for ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
			System.out.println("Invalid index! Please provide a valid index within the array bounds.");
		} catch (Exception e) {
			// Default exception handler
			System.out.println("An unexpected exception occurred!");
		}

        // Continue with the rest of the program...
	}
}
```

In this example, the try block attempts to retrieve a value from the `numbers` array at an index that is out of bounds (`index = 6`). Since this would throw an `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`, the first catch block is executed, printing the message "Invalid index! Please provide a valid index within the array bounds." 

Additionally, there is a second catch block that catches any other exceptions not specifically handled by the previous catch blocks. This catch block will handle any other exception types not explicitly caught, and in this case, it displays the message "An unexpected exception occurred!".

Note that the catch blocks are placed in the order of most specific to most general; this ensures that the specific exceptions are caught first before the more general catch block is reached. how to I put a tring to lowercase To convert a string to lowercase in Java, you can use the `toLowerCase()` method of the `String` class. Here's an example:

